---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import BurdenMap from "../components/BurdenMap.astro";
---
<Base description="How much of their income Virginia households spend on home energy, by county and city and by income level.">
  <section class="band">
    <h1>Below the poverty line, Virginia households spend about a fifth of their income on home energy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Energy burden is the share of household income spent on electricity, gas and other heating fuels. Spending 6% or more
      is generally considered unaffordable. The map shows how burden varies across Virginia's 133 counties and independent
      cities, by household income level.
    </p>
    <BurdenMap />
  </section>

  <section class="band notes">
    <div>
      <h3>How burden is calculated</h3>
      <p>Average burden follows the DOE LEAD Tool method: average annual energy cost (electricity, gas and other fuels) divided
      by average annual household income, weighted by households. "Share above 6%" is estimated from LEAD's household cells,
      grouped by tenure, building age, building type and heating fuel, and counts households in cells whose average burden is
      6% or more.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Data</h3>
      <p><a href="https://catalog.data.gov/dataset/low-income-energy-affordability-data-lead-tool-2022-update">DOE Low-Income
      Energy Affordability Data (LEAD) Tool, 2022 update</a>, county file by Federal Poverty Level (FPL), based on Census
      American Community Survey 5-year data. Boundaries: Census cartographic boundary file, 2023. Utilities serving each
      locality: <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/data/eia861/">EIA-861, 2024</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Limits</h3>
      <p>Figures are modeled estimates, not utility billing data, and reflect costs before the 2024–2026 rate increases. Small
      localities and the lowest income groups rest on few survey responses and can swing widely. Incomes under the poverty line
      are very low on average, so burden for that group is high everywhere.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .notes { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 320px), 1fr)); gap: 24px; font-size: 15px; color: var(--muted); }
  .notes h3 { margin: 0 0 6px; color: var(--ink); }
</style>
